{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "**Lost and Found in Japan**\n\nStepping off the plane in Tokyo, the first thing that hits you isn't the neon lights, but the silence. Despite the millions of people, there is a quiet orderliness that feels almost magical. My journey began in the heart of Shibuya, watching the famous scramble crossing. It was a symphony of movement, yet everyone seemed to move with purpose, never colliding.\n\nFrom the modern chaos of the capital, I traveled west to Kyoto. The energy shifted instantly. Here, time seemed to slow down amidst the ancient temples of Kinkaku-ji and the thousands of vermilion gates at Fushimi Inari. Walking through the bamboo groves of Arashiyama, the only sound was the rustling of leaves and the distant chime of a temple bell. It was a profound contrast to the digital hum of Tokyo, grounding me in history and nature.\n\nThe food alone is worth the trip. From steaming bowls of ramen in a tiny noodle shop to the delicate art of sushi at a counter in Osaka, every meal felt like a celebration. I still dream about the perfect balance of sweet and savory in a single piece of nigiri.\n\nJapan taught me that innovation and tradition can coexist beautifully. You can ride a bullet train that pulses with technology and then sit in a centuries-old garden that demands complete stillness. It is a place where you can get lost in a crowded city and find yourself in a quiet temple. If you ever have the chance to go, take it. Just remember to bring an empty stomach and an open heart.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.29092026}, {"token": "Lost", "logprob": -4.164678}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.6435032}, {"token": " Found", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.00066189014}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.8541123}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -0.0056357943}, {"token": "\n\n", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Ste", "logprob": -0.0